From a1aa14554c0de8808f32a7695888a31383ae3cbe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fernando Luiz Cola <fernando.luiz@pantacor.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 19:44:58 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] cmd: source: stop at mkimage's zero-terminated size table

Standard mkimage -T script images carry a multi-file size table
terminated by a plain 0, but this loop only stops at
IMAGE_PARAM_INVAL (0xffffffff), which never appears in such an
image. It therefore runs past the terminator and through the script
text until it happens upon 0xffffffff, leaving the command pointer
at an arbitrary offset -- U-Boot then reports
"Unknown command '<binary garbage>'" and the script never runs.

The comment directly above already describes the zero terminator, so
restore that behaviour while still accepting IMAGE_PARAM_INVAL for
any image that relies on it.

Signed-off-by: Fernando Luiz Cola <fernando.luiz@pantacor.com>
---
 cmd/source.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/cmd/source.c b/cmd/source.c
index 6b1c8b7..1852cc0 100644
--- a/cmd/source.c
+++ b/cmd/source.c
@@ -86,8 +86,14 @@ source (ulong addr, const char *fit_uname)
 		 * scripts are just multi-image files with one component, seek
 		 * past the zero-terminated sequence of image lengths to get
 		 * to the actual image data
+		 *
+		 * mkimage terminates this table with a plain 0, not
+		 * IMAGE_PARAM_INVAL; scanning only for the latter runs off the
+		 * end into the script text. Accept either terminator.
 		 */
-		while (*data++ != IMAGE_PARAM_INVAL);
+		while (*data != 0 && *data != IMAGE_PARAM_INVAL)
+			data++;
+		data++;
 		break;
 #endif
 #if defined(CONFIG_FIT)
-- 
2.43.0

